2026-10-14T02:22:45+02:00 PRE-REGISTRATION nap-model (written before any nap model was trained, decoded or scored and before any ends-in-wake window was built; full text also in tools/train/out/prereg-nap-model.md):
  # Pre-registration: a dedicated nap model (LOSO on nap proxies v2)
  
  Written 2026-10-14T02:22:45+02:00, BEFORE any nap model has been trained, decoded or scored and before any ends-in-wake window
  (kind b below) has been built: no candidate code has been written or run. Main checkout, branch main, base commit
  c2392ea (two processing modes, nap section of the bundle supported, no nap model shipped). Also appended verbatim to
  `tools/train/out/commands.log`. Known numbers: the published night-level results of ALGORITHM.md section 13 (runs
  `loso-v2`, `bias-base`, `bias-base/bias`, `bias-final`) and the nap-proxy results of section 13.11 (runs `naps`,
  `naps-posthoc`), i.e. the current default N0, RULES, HMM-table and "all LIGHT" on the onset proxies of 30, 60, 90,
  120, 150 and 170 min. Nothing is known about a nap model or about ends-in-wake windows.
  
  ## Question
  
  Naps (sessions shorter than `napMode.maxSessionMin` = 180 min) are decoded today by the night model with the
  short-session bias `stageLogBiasShort` (candidate C-180 of section 13.11): about half of the 30-s epochs agree with
  PSG, the stage shares are far off, and a 30-min nap is no better than "all LIGHT". Does a dedicated nap model (the
  bundle's optional `nap` section: classifier, durations, transitions, initial stage, time prior, class priors and its
  own stageLogBias, all fitted on nap-length sessions, with its own decoder parameters `napMode.hybrid.*`) do better,
  validated with the same rigour as the night model (leave one subject out, nested selection, pre-registered rules)?
  And, whatever is adopted, which nap output policy (STAGES / SLEEP_WAKE / SKIP) should be the default?
  
  ## Nap proxies v2
  
  From every OHealth-like night file of both headline variants (`tools/train/data/nights/{instantaneous,mean2min}/`;
  Walch = PSG, headline; BIDSleep = EEG headband, secondary), two kinds of proxy sessions:
  
  (a) ONSET proxies, exactly as in section 13.11 (`pulsetrain.naps.nap_proxy`, unchanged): session start s0 = the
  night's OHealth-like session start (first non-wake epoch - 5 min), end s1 = s0 + L, L in {30, 60, 90, 120, 150, 170}
  min; heart rate of [s0 - 30 min, s1], step records ending in (s0 - 30 min, s1], label epochs fully inside [s0, s1];
  NO data after s1; id `<night id>-nap<L>`. Every prepared session is at least 199 min long, so all exist.
  
  (b) ENDS-IN-WAKE windows. On the night's 30-s label epochs (inside its OHealth-like session), a wake bout is a
  maximal run of consecutive AWAKE epochs (each starting where the previous one ends; any other stage or a gap in the
  labels ends the run). For every wake bout o of at least 2 min (the opening bout), the closing bout c is the first wake
  bout of at least 5 min that starts at or after the end of o. The sleep segment is [end of o, start of c]; the proxy
  session puts the OHealth-like margins of `prepare.py` around it: s0 = end of o - min(5 min, length of o) (never more
  wake than the opening bout has), s1 = start of c + 5 min (the first 5 min of the closing awakening, so the session
  ends in wake as a real nap does). The window is kept when 30 min <= s1 - s0 <= 179 min (a nap under the 180-min
  threshold). Heart rate of [s0 - 30 min, s1] (up to 30 min of pre-session context, as far as the night file has data),
  step records ending in (s0 - 30 min, s1], label epochs fully inside [s0, s1], NO data after s1 (cut by the same code
  as (a)); id `<night id>-ew<m>` with m = (s0 - night session start) in minutes (`f"{m:g}"`). Deterministic sampling,
  capped per night: the night's windows are ordered by SHA-256 of `<night key>|<s0 - night session start in ms>` (night
  key = the night id without its variant suffix `-inst` / `-mean2min`, so both variants get the same windows), and
  taken greedily in that order when they do not overlap a window already taken, at most K = 3 per night.
  
  Why both kinds: a real nap starts after wakefulness and ends with an awakening. (a) has the first property (sleep
  onset after wake, the stage sequence of sleep onset) but ends inside sleep; (b) has the second (the session ends 5 min
  into an awakening of at least 5 min, the end that the model's time-to-end features should learn for naps) but starts
  after a nocturnal awakening. Neither alone is a nap; both together cover the two ends of one. Stated limitations
  (reported with the results): no afternoon circadian phase (both kinds are night-time sleep, (a) in the evening after a
  full day awake, (b) in the middle or late part of the night with sleep pressure partly dissipated and more REM); no
  real post-nap wake heart rate or steps (no data after the end); the pre-session context of (b) is nocturnal sleep or
  wake, not daytime activity; (a)'s session start is the PSG sleep onset - 5 min (a watch may detect a nap late); the
  onset proxies of one night overlap (the first 30 min appear in all six lengths); 31 young healthy adults in one lab
  (Walch) and 47 adults at home with headband labels (BIDSleep).
  
  ## Data, folds and the artefacts of the current default
  
  - Pool = all 78 subjects (31 Walch + 47 BIDSleep), training configuration `walch+bid-zero` (BIDSleep step features
    exactly as algo-cli computes them: 0 steps). Every subject is held out once (LOSO by subject: all proxies of the
    held-out subject, of both kinds and from all its nights, are excluded from everything fitted for it).
  - Outer groups and inner folds: those of run `bias-base` (`--selection per-group`, 5 dataset-stratified groups, seed
    0): the selection set of a group g is the night run's `selection/<key>.json` `subjects` (the pool minus g's
    subjects), its inner folds are that file's `innerFolds`, inner scoring on its Walch subjects. The same groups in both
    variants.
  - N0 at fold level (the current main behaviour): each held-out subject's outer fold night bundle of `bias-base`
    (`hybrid/walch+bid-zero/<variant>/mlp/folds/<subject>.json`) + the fold-level stageLogBias b_g
    (`bias-base/bias/decisions.json`, candidate B) + the fold-level stageLogBiasShort b_short_g(180) (`naps/fits.json`,
    T = 180), no nap section, decoder theta_g of the night run for nights and naps, beta = 1.
  - Reproduction gate, before any nap model is fitted: with the algo-cli build of the commit that holds this
    pre-registration and its analysis code (fingerprint recorded), N0 must reproduce (1) every whole night of both
    variants (568 decodes) as `bias-base/bias/results.jsonl` candidate B and (2) every onset proxy of 30-120 min (2272
    decodes) as candidate C-180 of `naps/results.jsonl` (confusion matrix, estimated or not, warnings; the `SANITY_*`
    codes of sessions shorter than 180 min are absent in this build, `naps.expected_warnings`); the 150 / 170-min onset
    proxies are compared with `naps-posthoc` and reported. If (1) or (2) fails: stop and document.
  - Feature parity, before any fit: the features of the onset proxies cut in Python must equal (every field) the rows
    that `algo-cli features --nap-proxies 30,60,90,120,150,170` cuts in Kotlin for the same nights; the feature set must
    be `FEATURE_SET_VERSION` 1 with the 40 names of `algo-cli feature-names`. Every training matrix passes
    `loso.check_train_inference_parity` (the training values are the algo-cli values of the same epochs) and every
    exported classifier reproduces sklearn to 1e-9 (`models.verify_export`).
  
  ## Candidates for nap sessions (all proxies are naps: shorter than 180 min)
  
  - N0: current main behaviour, as above (night model + stageLogBiasShort).
  - N1: dedicated nap model, per held-out subject s of outer group g and variant v:
    - training rows: the epochs with heart-rate data and a label of all proxies (both kinds) of the training subjects
      (pool minus s), from BOTH variants (instantaneous rows, then mean2min rows), the 40 features as algo-cli computes
      them for the proxy sessions;
    - classifier: multinomial logistic regression (lbfgs, L2, C in {0.01, 0.1, 1} chosen by inner log-loss as in
      `loso.run_selection`) or an MLP 2 x 32 (relu, adam, alpha 1e-4, batch 256, early stopping on 10 %, seed 0), the
      type (and C) chosen per (variant, g) in the nested inner CV below;
    - nap-specific statistics fitted on the training subjects' proxies of variant v (`stats.fit_label_stats`, the
      night protocol's settings): gamma bout durations, bout transitions, initial stage, time prior P(stage | 30-min bin
      since the session start), class priors = the classifier's training-row frequencies;
    - nap stageLogBias b_nap_g: the fixed point of `pulsetrain/bias.py` (`fit_stage_bias`, unchanged: start 0, eta 1
      halved on a non-improving step, at most 8 decodes, stop at D <= 0.005) per (variant, g) on the training folds
      only: the inner out-of-fold decodes (all proxies of both kinds) of g's inner held-out Walch selection subjects,
      each by its inner fold nap model, at g's chosen classifier and decoder parameters;
    - decoder parameters theta_nap_g = (durationModel, emissionWeight, timePriorWeight), set as `napMode.hybrid.*`;
    - the nap model goes into the `nap` section of s's N0 fold bundle (main part unchanged), so the evaluation runs the
      code path the app runs.
  - N2: N1 without the nap stageLogBias (same classifier, statistics and theta_nap_g).
  - Nested inner CV per (variant v, group g), never touching g's subjects: for each inner fold, LR (each C) and the MLP
    are fitted on the inner training subjects' proxies (both variants), with statistics from v; one
    `algo-cli evaluate --param-grid` per classifier scores the full grid durationModel {EXPLICIT, GEOMETRIC} x
    emissionWeight {1, 0.7, 0.5, 0.3} x timePriorWeight {1, 0.5, 2, 0} (32 points, the order of `loso.GRIDS["full"]`)
    as `napMode.hybrid` patches on the inner held-out Walch subjects' proxies (both kinds, variant v), each decoded by
    its inner fold nap model (a bundle whose main model and nap section are both that model: every evaluated session is
    a nap, so only the nap section decodes). Inner score = mean
    per-subject Cohen's kappa (AWAKE / LIGHT / DEEP / REM; each subject's proxies of both kinds pooled; the night
    protocol's score). The (classifier, grid point) with the highest inner score is chosen; ties -> logreg before MLP,
    then grid order.
  - Context (not candidates): RULES (AlgoParams defaults) and HMM-table (the physiology table,
    `loso.physiology_hmm_patch`) as the nap estimator, untrained, on the same proxies; "all LIGHT" (every scored chunk
    of N0 predicted LIGHT, from the labels); and a SLEEP_WAKE evaluation of N0 and N1 (N2 and the context estimators also
    reported).
  - Common evaluation parameters: the night run's base parameters, `hybrid` = theta_g of the night run and beta 1,
    `napMode.maxSessionMin` 180, `napMode.estimator` HYBRID, `napMode.outputPolicy` STAGES (the stages are scored; the
    policy is applied after the estimate), `napMode.post` defaults (no stage-share checks on naps), `napMode.hybrid` =
    theta_nap_g (N1, N2) or theta_g of the night run (N0).
  - Nights unchanged at fold level: the N1 and N2 evaluations include the held-out subjects' whole nights, which must
    reproduce candidate B of `bias-base/bias/results.jsonl` night by night (568 of 568); otherwise stop and document.
  
  ## Metrics
  
  Per session, on its scored 30-s chunks (prediction at the chunk midpoint; chunks predicted UNKNOWN in long heart-rate
  gaps are not scored): confusion matrix, accuracy, true and predicted shares, summed share error E = 100 * sum over
  AWAKE, LIGHT, DEEP, REM of |P_s - T_s| (percentage points, 0-200), kappa, flags, NOT_ESTIMATED reason.
  
  Pooled over the proxies of both kinds (per candidate, dataset and variant; the rules use Walch):
  - accuracy = correct / scored chunks over all scored chunks of all proxies of both kinds (one pooled matrix);
  - E = mean over all proxies (sessions) of the per-session E;
  - 4-class kappa = mean per-subject Cohen's kappa, each subject's proxies of both kinds pooled (subjects where defined);
  - sleep/wake (SLEEP_WAKE output: AWAKE stays AWAKE, LIGHT / DEEP / REM become "sleeping"; every 4 x 4 matrix
    collapsed to 2 x 2 wake / sleep): sleep/wake kappa = mean per-subject Cohen's kappa of the collapsed matrices (each
    subject's proxies pooled; where defined), wake F1 and sleep/wake accuracy of the pooled collapsed matrix;
  - "all LIGHT" accuracy = the LIGHT share of the true labels of the same scored chunks.
  Also reported, not used: the same per kind (onset / ends-in-wake), per onset length, per ends-in-wake length bin
  (30-59, 60-89, 90-119, 120-149, 150-179 min), per-session mean accuracy, chunk-pooled kappas, shares, flags,
  NOT_ESTIMATED counts, the inner choices and scores per group, the b fits, BIDSleep (secondary) for everything.
  Paired per-subject bootstrap (`report.paired_bootstrap`, 10 000 resamples of subjects, seed 0, 95 % percentile CI) of
  N1 - N0, N2 - N0, N1 - N2 and every context estimator - N0, per dataset and variant, pooled and per kind: accuracy
  (subject value = the subject's pooled accuracy), E (mean over the subject's proxies), 4-class kappa, sleep/wake kappa,
  wake F1 (subjects where defined in both); and candidate - "all LIGHT" accuracy for the policy rule.
  
  ## Rule (i): the model (verbatim)
  
  "On Walch, worse sampling variant, pooled over proxies of both kinds: adopt the nap-model candidate with the highest
  accuracy among those whose summed share error <= N0's and whose accuracy >= N0's + 0.02; else keep N0."
  
  Operational definitions: nap-model candidates = N1, N2; accuracy worse = min and E worse = max over {instantaneous,
  mean2min} of the pooled values above (each criterion on its own worse variant); qualify = E worse <= E worse of N0
  and accuracy worse >= accuracy worse of N0 + 0.02; ties in accuracy (within 0.0005) -> lower E worse, then N1 before
  N2. Keeping N0 means: no nap section is installed.
  
  ## Rule (ii): the default output policy for naps (verbatim)
  
  "STAGES if the adopted candidate's accuracy >= 'all LIGHT' accuracy + 0.03 AND its 4-class kappa >= 0.15; otherwise
  SLEEP_WAKE if its sleep/wake kappa >= 0.20; otherwise SKIP."
  
  Operational definitions: the adopted candidate X of rule (i) (N0 if kept); Walch, pooled over proxies of both kinds;
  accuracy margin = min over the two variants of (accuracy of X - "all LIGHT" accuracy on X's scored chunks of that
  variant); 4-class kappa and sleep/wake kappa = min over the two variants of the mean per-subject values above. The
  result becomes `TrainedDefaults.NAP_OUTPUT_POLICY`, the default of `napMode.outputPolicy`.
  
  ## If a nap model (N1 or N2) is adopted: final model and installation
  
  - Final classifier type = the most frequent choice over the 156 outer folds (78 subjects x 2 variants, each fold
    carrying its group's choice; ties -> higher mean inner score, then logreg); C = the most frequent C of the folds
    with that type; theta_nap = the most frequent theta_nap_g of those folds (ties -> higher mean inner score, then
    grid order).
  - Final nap model fitted on the proxies of all 78 subjects (rows of both variants, instantaneous first), statistics
    from the instantaneous proxies (labels are identical in the two variants).
  - N1 only: final nap stageLogBias = the same fixed point on the LOSO out-of-fold decodes of all proxies (both kinds)
    of the 31 Walch subjects, both variants pooled, each decoded by its outer fold nap model of the final type and C
    without b (refitted on the same training subjects where its group chose another type or C) at theta_nap.
  - Installed as the `nap` section of the shipped bundle (`naps-final/final/hybrid_model.json`, main model unchanged)
    into `app/src/main/assets/models/hybrid_model.json`; `TrainedDefaults` regenerated: `NAP_HYBRID_DURATION_MODEL`,
    `NAP_HYBRID_EMISSION_WEIGHT`, `NAP_HYBRID_TIME_PRIOR_WEIGHT` = theta_nap and `NAP_OUTPUT_POLICY` = rule (ii). If N0
    is kept: no nap section, `NAP_HYBRID_*` stay null, only `NAP_OUTPUT_POLICY` is set by rule (ii).
  
  ## Verification: nights unchanged (with the final build)
  
  After the new defaults and bundle are built into algo-cli: (1) the fold bundles of the adopted candidate (N0 bundles
  plus its nap section, or N0's) decoded with each group's night decoder theta_g and otherwise the build's DEFAULT
  parameters (`napMode` not passed) reproduce candidate B of `bias-base/bias/results.jsonl` on all 568 whole nights; (2)
  the adopted candidate's proxy decodes are reproduced session by session (its theta_nap_g and `outputPolicy` STAGES
  passed explicitly); (3) the installed bundle and the previous shipped bundle
  decode all 568 whole nights with the default parameters identically (confusion matrix, warnings, estimated status and
  the per-epoch output); (4) the installed bundle is accepted on every proxy (HYBRID used; resubstitution, not a
  validation result). Any failure is documented and blocks the installation.
  
  ## Commands, outputs, provenance
  
  ```
  mise exec -- ./gradlew :algo-cli:installDist    # the build of the commit with this file and its analysis code
  JAVA_OPTS=-Xmx2g mise exec -- python -I tools/train/train.py nap-model --out-run nap-model --jobs 16
  mise exec -- python -I tools/train/train.py nap-model-report --out-run nap-model
  JAVA_OPTS=-Xmx2g mise exec -- python -I tools/train/train.py nap-model-final --exp nap-model --out-run nap-model-final --install-model
  mise exec -- python -I tools/train/train.py defaults --run nap-model-final --write
  mise exec -- ./gradlew :algo-cli:installDist
  JAVA_OPTS=-Xmx2g mise exec -- python -I tools/train/train.py nap-model-verify --exp nap-model --final nap-model-final --out-run nap-model-verify
  ```
  
  Code: `tools/train/pulsetrain/napmodel.py`, `train.py nap-model | nap-model-report | nap-model-final |
  nap-model-verify`, tests in `tools/train/tests/`. This file, its SHA-256 and the analysis code are committed before
  the first algo-cli call of the experiment; every later change to the analysis code is logged in
  `tools/train/out/commands.log` and disclosed in ALGORITHM.md section 13.12, which documents the protocol, all tables,
  both decisions and a plain conclusion whatever the outcome. `--jobs` changes no result (fixed seeds, single-threaded
  BLAS).
